{{see also|Florida's 1st congressional district}}
The incumbent was Republican [[Matt Gaetz]], who was re-elected with 67.9% of the vote in 2022.<ref name="HPVT">{{cite web|url=https://www.cookpolitical.com/charts/house-charts/national-house-vote-tracker/2022|title=2022 National House Vote Tracker|access-date=November 28, 2023|website=[[The Cook Political Report with Amy Walter|Cook Political Report]]}}</ref>

Although Gaetz won re-election, just over a week later, he resigned from Congress on November 13 after being nominated to become [[United States Attorney General|U.S. attorney general]] under [[Donald Trump]].<ref>{{cite web |date=November 13, 2024 |title=Gaetz resigns from Congress — possibly skirting long-awaited Ethics report |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2024/11/13/matt-gaetz-resigns-congress-00189488 |access-date=November 14, 2024 |website=Politico}}</ref> However, he withdrew from the nomination a week later, on November 21, due to controversy.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Tucker |first1=Eric |last2=Durkin Richer |first2=Alanna |date=November 21, 2024 |title=Gaetz withdraws as Trump's pick for attorney general, averting confirmation battle in the Senate |url=https://apnews.com/article/gaetz-trump-fbi-justice-department-248b46ba0c882dd46d661568e8bd3bd7? |access-date=November 21, 2024 |work=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref>

A special election was held on April 1, 2025, to replace Gaetz.<ref>{{cite web |title=Special Election set to replace Matt Gaetz |url=https://floridapolitics.com/archives/707886-special-elections-cd1-cd6/ |publisher=[[Florida Politics]] |access-date=November 23, 2024 |date=November 22, 2024}}</ref>

